# Mutation cases for W3C SCXML 3.10 + 3.11 — a `<history>` restores the cycle
# to where it was, not to the top of the loop.
#
# Run with `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/ai_loop_history_cpp.cases`.
#
# `examples/ai_loop/ai_loop.scxml` carries `<history id="where">` inside the
# turn cycle so that a person who holds the run to look at the work resumes it
# where it stopped.
#
# An earlier revision of this comment claimed the W3C corpus could not see the
# clause at all — that it reaches history only through documents whose
# restoration target is also their initial state, where a history recording
# nothing is indistinguishable from one that works. That is FALSE, and it was
# inherited rather than measured. Breaking the filter and running the corpus
# reds W3C **388, 579 and 580**, on both engines. The clause is guarded, and
# guarded on every backend, since the W3C suite runs on all of them.
#
# What this file therefore pins is narrower and worth stating honestly: the
# same filter under a `<parallel>` with three regions and a hold taken away
# from the history default — the shape `ai_loop` has and the corpus does not.
# It is a second sampling of one clause, not the only one.
#
# The runner is the integration suite rather than the example, and the reason is
# a measurement. `ai_loop_example` runs four scripted runs and checks that each
# ends somewhere the document enumerates — and an outcome cannot see a
# configuration. Measured 2026-08-13: both cases below SURVIVED against
# `-R '^ai_loop_example$'`, because a run whose `<history>` records nothing
# still converges. `tests/integration/AiLoopAotTest.cpp` asks the per-clause
# question instead, and `ResumeReturnsSomewhereTheHistoryDefaultDoesNot` is the
# assertion written for this one: it holds the cycle in `priming`, which is the
# single place where a working history and the history state's own
# `<transition target="working"/>` default give different answers.
#
# The helper below is shared between the Interpreter and the AOT engines
# (`ai_loop_sm.inl` calls it by name), so a defect restored here is a defect in
# both. Its Rust sibling is `ai_loop_history_rust.cases`, which mutates the
# committed generated tree because that backend inlines the same filter instead
# of calling a helper.

mutation_ctest --test-dir build -R '^IntegrationTests$'
mutation_targets sce/include/core/HistoryHelper.h

# ── Shallow history records nothing ────────────────────────────────
#
# §scxml-3.10 has a shallow `<history>` record the ACTIVE CHILDREN of its
# parent. Recording an empty set is the shape a filter takes when its parent
# comparison is wrong in a way that never matches: entry then falls back to the
# parent's `initial`, so `resume` lands at the top of the cycle instead of
# where the person paused it. Nothing crashes and no state is left invalid,
# which is why only a document that pauses somewhere OTHER than its initial
# state can tell the difference.

mutation_case "shallow history records no state at all" <<'PY'
edit("sce/include/core/HistoryHelper.h",
     "        if (parent.has_value() && parent.value() == parentState) {",
     "        if (false && parent.has_value() && parent.value() == parentState) {")
PY

# ── Shallow history records the wrong states ───────────────────────
#
# The opposite error: keep everything EXCEPT the parent's direct children. A
# comparison inverted by one character produces it, and in a three-region
# `<parallel>` what gets recorded is most of the rest of the configuration —
# states in regions the history state has no business naming. Restoration then
# re-enters them, so the run comes back from a hold in a configuration no
# transition produced.

mutation_case "shallow history records everything but the direct children" <<'PY'
edit("sce/include/core/HistoryHelper.h",
     "        if (parent.has_value() && parent.value() == parentState) {",
     "        if (parent.has_value() && parent.value() != parentState) {")
PY
